  "strategy": "# (1) Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to model the optimization as a physical process where the function \nis treated like a distribution of forces and positions that minimize potential energy.\nThe challenge is converting a discrete step function to a configuration where the overlap \nwith its shifts is minimized. We combine:\n  - Probability-based hill climbing\n  - Local repair mechanisms for constraint violations\n  - Coarse-to-fine optimization schedules\n  - Progressive resolution refinement\n\nBottleneck addressed: \n- Overlapped distribution constraint is both binary/discrete and continuous\n- Non-convex optimization landscape\n- Slow convergence in multi-modal spaces\n\n# (2) Mathematical specification\nGoal: Minimize max_lag(\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx) over k\nkey transformation: The continuous problem is discretized using piecewise constant functions.\nVariables: Sequence h[i] for i in [0, n-1] with sum(h)=n/2 and 0<=h[i]<=1\nObjective: Evaluate the discrete convolution\nconstraint: sum(h) = n/2\n\n# (3) Implementation procedure\nstep 1: Validation\n  - Input: initial_h_values (n=70), current best known lower bound\n  - Validate coefficients and dimensions maintain physical interpretability\n\nstep 2: Preparation\n  - Detect problem parameters: dx=2/n, target_sum=n/2\n  - Advanced repair: Introduce jitter to maintain diversity in perturbations\n\nstep 3: Repair mechanism for active balance\n  - Maintain energy balance using a gradient projection approach\n  - Ensure integral is n/2 post-perturbation\n\nstep 4: Clustering initialization\n  - Group points based on function value to preserve signal structure\n  - Quantize potential candidates to enforce sparse updates\n\nstep 5: Optimization algorithm\n  - Metropolis acceptance criterion with decay schedule\n  - Energy function: -log( sum_{k} exp(-overlap^2/kT) )\n  - Parallel rollout of multiple temperature profiles\n\nstep 6: Objective evaluation\n  - Fast integer dot product for convolution approximation\n  - Includes gradient step using central finite differences\n\n# (4) Search configuration\nmutation_rate = 0.3\ntemperature_start = 1.5\nannealing_schedule = 'exp'\ninitial_range = [0.1, 0.9]\nresolution_boost_threshold = 0.0\n\n# (5) Computation profiling\n  - Convolution: O(n^2), n=70 => around 5000 operations per evaluation\n  - Annealing with interpolation parameter which decays\n  - Each simulation step averages 3 operations\n\n  Schedule:\n    900 seconds: Main simulation phase\n    100 seconds: Cooldown and refinement\n    100 seconds: Resolution boost if significant improvement\n\n# (6) Validation and return\n  - Feasibility: \n    * 0<=h[i]<=1 for all i\n    * sum(h) == n/2 (within floating point)\n  - Metric consistency: Compute maximum overlap using original scoring function\n  - Output format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
